\[H_{0}({\rm div};\Omega)=\{{\bf v}\in L^{2}(\Omega)^{2};\,{\rm div}\,{\bf v}\in L^{ 2}(\Omega),\,{\bf v}\cdot{\bf n}|_{\Gamma}=0\},\]

then the sum of the surface integrals: \(\sum_{{\bf v}\in{\mathscr{T}}_{h}}\int_{\partial\kappa}{\bf v}\cdot{\bf n}M_{n}( \tau)\,ds\) is zero. Therefore the assumption that \({\bf u}\) belongs to \(H^{2}(\Omega)^{2}\) permits to apply (4.5) to the first equations (4.2), thus giving:

\[v\sum_{{\bf v}\in{\mathscr{T}}_{h}}\biggl{\{}\int_{\kappa}\lambda_ {ij}(\partial v_{i}/\partial x_{j})\,dx-\int_{\partial\kappa}M_{nt}(\lambda){ \bf v}\cdot{\bf t}\,ds\biggr{\}}-\int_{\Omega}p\,{\rm div}\,{\bf v}\,dx=\int_{ \Omega}{\bf f}\cdot{\bf v}\,dx\] (4.6) \[\forall{\bf v}\in H_{0}({\rm div};\Omega)\quad\mbox{with }{\bf v}|_{\kappa}\in H^{1}(\kappa)^{2}\qquad\forall\kappa\in{\mathscr{T}}_{h}.\]

Likewise, to match (4.6) we can also express the relation (4.1) between \({\bf u}\) and \(\lambda\) by:

\[\int_{\Omega}\lambda_{ij}\tau_{ij}\,dx=\sum_{{\bf v}\in{\mathscr{T}}_{h}} \biggl{\{}\int_{\kappa}\tau_{ij}(\partial u_{i}/\partial x_{j})\,dx-\int_{ \partial\kappa}M_{nt}(\tau){\bf u}\cdot{\bf t}\,ds\biggr{\}}\qquad\forall \tau\in H^{1}(\Omega)^{4}\] (4.7)

since the sum

\[\sum_{{\bf v}\in{\mathscr{T}}_{h}}\int_{\partial\kappa}M_{nt}(\tau){\bf u} \cdot{\bf t}\,ds\]

is zero for \({\bf u}\) in \(H_{0}^{1}(\Omega)^{2}\) and \(\tau\) in \(H^{1}(\Omega)^{4}\).

Finally a look at (4.6) and (4.7) shows that while the regularity of the test function \({\bf v}\) can hardly be decreased, the test tensor \(\tau\) need not belong globally to \(H^{1}(\Omega)^{4}\). In fact, (4.7) makes sense if \(\tau\) belongs to \(H^{1}(\kappa)^{4}\) on each \(\kappa\). Besides that, it is convenient to assume that \(M_{nt}(\tau)\) is continuous across interelement boundaries--i.e. across all segments of \(\Gamma_{h}\).

Summing up, we see that if \(({\bf u},p)\) is the solution of the Stokes problem (2.1) with \({\bf u}\) in \((H^{2}(\Omega)\cap H_{0}^{\downarrow}(\Omega))^{2}\) and \(p\) in \(H^{1}(\Omega)\cap L_{0}^{2}(\Omega)\) then the triple \(({\bf u},\lambda,p)\) with

\[\lambda_{ij}=\partial u_{i}/\partial x_{j}\]

is also a solution of:

\[\begin{cases}{\bf u}\in H_{0}({\rm div};\Omega)\quad\mbox{with }{\bf u}|_{\kappa}\in H^{1}(\kappa)^{2}\qquad\forall\kappa\in{\mathscr{T}}_{ h},\\ \lambda\in H^{1}(\kappa)^{4}\qquad\forall\kappa\in{\mathscr{T}}_{h}\quad\mbox{ with }M_{nt}(\lambda)\mbox{ continuous on }\Gamma_{h}\quad\mbox{and}\\ \lambda_{11}+\lambda_{22}=0,\qquad p\in L_{0}^{2}(\Omega),\\ v\sum_{{\bf x}\in{\mathscr{T}}_{h}}\biggl{\{}\int_{\kappa}\lambda_{ij}( \partial v_{i}/\partial x_{j})\,dx-\int_{\partial\kappa}M_{nt}(\lambda){\bf v }\cdot{\bf t}\,ds\biggr{\}}-\int_{\Omega}p\,{\rm div}\,{\bf v}\,dx\\ =\int_{\Omega}{\bf f}\cdot{\bf v}\,dx\qquad\forall{\bf v}\in H_{0}({ \rm div};\Omega)\quad\mbox{with }{\bf v}|_{\kappa}\in H^{1}(\kappa)^{2}\qquad\forall \kappa\in{\mathscr{T}}_{h},\\ \int_{\Omega}\lambda_{ij}\tau_{ij}\,dx-\sum_{{\bf x}\in{\mathscr{T}}_{h}} \biggl{\{}\int_{\kappa}\tau_{ij}(\partial u_{i}/\partial x_{j})\,dx-\int_{ \partial\kappa}M_{nt}(\tau){\bf u}\cdot{\bf t}\,ds\biggr{\}}=0\\ \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\forall\tau\in H^{1}(\kappa)^{4}\qquad\forall\kappa\in{ \mathscr{T}}_{h}\quad\mbox{with }M_{nt}(\tau)\mbox{ continuous on }\Gamma_{h}.\end{cases}\] (4.8)

